entries.english:
Franz Ludwig [von Erthal], Bischof zu Bamberg und Würzburg, following the death of his predecessor Adam Friedrich [von Seinsheim], grants Johann Friedrich Karl Philipp von Rosenbach the following properties as fiefs held by male heirs:
- Thundorf Castle with all rights, appurtenances, and revenues, except only the farmyard;
- the deserted settlement of Ransbach (Ramspach), with its mill and farm and all rights and revenues there;
- the villages of Theinfeld and Rothhausen, with all rights, properties, and revenues there;
- an estate in Maßbach behind the churchyard;
- the properties in Poppenlauer;
- the properties in Ebertshausen;
- half of the tithe at Altenmünster (Münster on the Schlettag);
- one third and one eighth of the tithe at Oberlauringen;
- one eighth of the tithe at Alsleben;
- one quarter of the tithe at Ober- and Untereßfeld;
- a vineyard and two woodlands in Thundorf.
The bishop further grants them the following properties, which had belonged outright to Veit Ulrich von Schaumberg and which he then enfeoffed to the Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg:
- the farmyard and vineyards in Thundorf;
- the lake at Weißensee;
- a fishery in the Lauer below Maßbach;
- a fishery in the Lauer within the Rothhausen district;
- three quarters of the mill at Rothhausen;
- two estates at Theinfeld;
- two freehold estates at Weichtungen;
- four rent fiefs at Stadtlauringen;
- nine estates and eleven fiefs in Maßbach;
- the Brand mill below Maßbach;
- five estates and four fiefs in Poppenlauer;
- three estates and one fief in Nüdlingen;
- one fief in Rannungen;
- a field estate in Hesselbach;
- the Herlingsberg woodland;
- the fief held by male heirs at Prappach.
These properties were purchased from the brothers Georg Dietrich, Wilhelm Ludwig, Wilhelm Ulrich, Georg Wilhelm, Johann Sigmund, Johann Philipp, and Johann Wilhelm von Schaumberg.
Finally, the bishop also grants them the following properties, originally held in fief from the County of Henneberg and then from the Duchy of Saxe-Weimar, which passed to Würzburg under an agreement between the Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg and the Duchy of Saxe-Weimar:
- the cent court at Maßbach, subsequently moved to Poppenlauer, with all appurtenances and rights, excluding the villages of Maßbach and Volkershausen;
- half of the bailiwick in Poppenlauer;
- two estates in Maßbach and a meadow between Maßbach and Rothhausen;
- one quarter of the tithe at Zeil am Main;
- one third of a small quarter of the wine tithe at Zeil am Main;
- one third and one quarter of a full quarter of the wine tithe at Zeil am Main;
- four woodlands near Maßbach;
- the wine rents and interest revenues in Schonungen. If the von Rosenbach family should ever have no male heirs, the Prince-Bishopric of Würzburg is obliged to pay the family's female heirs 2,000 Reichstaler in consideration of these revenues.
Seal: The bishop with his secret seal.

Notes: Abstract (18th century)
